      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The lake’s name derives from the Abenaki word “Arcenciel,” meaning “Bow in the Sky,” a name the early hunters adopted for its shimmering waters.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://rainbowlakeassn.org/index.php/local-interest/61-history-of-rainbow-lake",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Until the mid‑20th century, a New York Central railroad station at Rainbow Lake brought vacationers and freight to the hamlet’s doorstep.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://wiki.historicsaranaclake.org/index.php/Rainbow_Lake",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rainbow Lake lies 9.7 miles north of Saranac Lake, placing it deep within the Adirondack Mountains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rainbow_Lake%2C_New_York",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Today the Kushaqua Dam keeps Rainbow Lake’s water level close to its original height, preserving the historic shoreline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://rainbowlakeassn.org/index.php/local-interest/61-history-of-rainbow-lake",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Before the dam, the area was a tamarack swamp fed by springs; flooding it created a clear lake with a solitary island.",
